# gitleaks ignore list — known false positives only.
#
# Format is gitleaks' own fingerprint: <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>
# A fingerprint pins ONE finding in ONE commit, so an entry here can never
# blind the scanner to a different secret, a different file, or a future
# commit. That narrowness is the whole point — see the reasoning below.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# backend/tests/test_password_leakage.py
#
# The value matched by generic-api-key is a CANARY used by the tests that prove
# a registration password is never persisted, logged or traced. It is not a
# credential and never was. gitleaks matches the `PASSWORD = "<long value>"`
# shape.
#
# The current line also carries an inline `# gitleaks:allow`, which stops the
# finding being reported for new commits. That is not enough on its own: once a
# value has been committed, adding a later fix does not remove it from history,
# and gitleaks correctly keeps reporting the original commit. This entry
# records that specific historical finding as reviewed and benign.
#
# Deliberately NOT used: a path allowlist. Allowlisting the whole file would
# silently blind the scanner to a real secret added to it later — the exact
# silent-failure mode this project is trying to avoid.
#
# Verified: with this file present, a realistic AWS key committed to a
# different file is still reported (aws-access-token) while this finding is
# suppressed. Exactly one finding is removed, none added.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
